+# Backing up and restoring run history
+
+Run history lives in one JSON file: `data/run-history.json` by default, or
+`RUN_HISTORY_FILE`. In Docker it is `/app/data/run-history.json` on the
+`stellarmind_data` volume. A backup is a single JSON file holding the history's
+exact bytes, its schema version, run and payment-proof counts, and a SHA-256 of
+the bytes.
+
+Stop the server before backing up or restoring: the tool drains only its own
+queued writes, not a running server's.
+
+## Local storage
+
+```sh
+node src/storage/history-backup-cli.js backup data/run-history.json backups/history.json
+node src/storage/history-backup-cli.js verify backups/history.json
+node src/storage/history-backup-cli.js restore backups/history.json data/run-history.json --replace
+```
+
+Each command prints the run and payment-proof counts, so you can compare them
+before and after a restore.
+
+## Container storage
+
+```sh
+docker compose stop stellarmind
+docker compose run --rm -v "$PWD/backups:/backups" stellarmind \
+  node src/storage/history-backup-cli.js backup /app/data/run-history.json /backups/history.json
+docker compose run --rm -v "$PWD/backups:/backups" stellarmind \
+  node src/storage/history-backup-cli.js restore /backups/history.json /app/data/run-history.json --replace
+docker compose start stellarmind
+```
+
+## What restore will and won't do
+
+- It verifies the checksum, the schema version and the counts before writing anything.
+  A damaged backup, or one from a newer schema, is refused and nothing changes.
+- It never replaces an existing history unless you pass `--replace`. When you do, the
+  old file is first copied to `run-history.json.pre-restore.<time>`.
